<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Tauri merges this into the generated Info.plist rather than replacing it,
      so only the keys Tauri does not already set belong here.

      These strings are shown verbatim in the system permission prompts, so they
      are product copy, not boilerplate. Apple rejects vague ones, and a missing
      NSMicrophoneUsageDescription terminates the process outright the first
      time the mic is touched.
    -->
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aura needs your microphone so you can talk to Buddy and use dictation. Audio is streamed only while you are speaking to Aura.</string>

    <!--
      System audio recording, for Meeting Notes. This is a REAL key, not a
      defensive one: the Core Audio process-tap API refuses to prompt without
      it, and the prompt only fires when AudioDeviceStart runs on the
      tap-backed aggregate device - there is no requestAuthorization to call
      earlier. That is also why bundle.macOS.minimumSystemVersion is 14.4
      rather than the 14.2 the API first shipped in: the TCC category behind
      this string only behaves correctly from 14.4.
    -->
    <key>NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aura records the audio of meetings you choose to capture, so Buddy can write your notes. Recording only ever starts on a meeting you armed yourself.</string>

    <!--
      Screen recording is granted through TCC, not through an entitlement, and
      Apple does not document a usage-description key for it. This string is
      included defensively: it is harmless if unused, and several capture
      frameworks surface it in the prompt.
    -->
    <key>NSScreenCaptureUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aura reads your screen only when you use Screen Sight or Guide Mode, so Buddy can see what you are working on.</string>

    <!--
      Apple Events, NOT Accessibility (Accessibility has no usage string; its
      prompt is the system's own). After a voice web search, dictation asks
      System Events to place the browser window on half the screen and asks
      Finder for the desktop size (dictation/command_brain.rs). This string is
      the "Aura wants to control System Events" prompt.
    -->
    <key>NSAppleEventsUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Aura places the browser window it opened for you on half of your screen, so you can see your search next to what you were doing.</string>

    <!--
      No LSUIElement. The app is a Regular activation policy app so it has a
      Dock icon and a Cmd+Tab entry; see lib.rs's set_activation_policy call
      for why that does not affect the overlay's focus behaviour. Declaring
      LSUIElement would still work - a runtime setActivationPolicy(Regular)
      brings the Dock icon back, which is how menu bar apps toggle it - but the
      app would launch as an Accessory and only gain the icon once setup runs,
      so the icon would visibly arrive late. Absent here, it is Regular from
      process start.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
